\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S13}{\ignorespaces Where each rig's cost saving goes on the forecast result's plan, which weighs the wind error on a grid fine near the ship, re-planned after each edge of about a day on the archived forecasts and sailed at the plan's speed, for the \SI {140}{m} e-ammonia ship to Guadeloupe, and the kite's excess over the wing sails and over the rotors, paired per departure, each with its standard error over 24 departures. The rotors pair 22 of them, since two of their round trips found no route. Cost savings are against the ship without a rig on the same plan. The loss is split by changing one thing at a time: from perfect foresight on the corridor of the results to perfect foresight on the planning grid, then to the forecast; the forecast's loss by what it gets wrong, the wave or the wind forecast first; and by what the plan decides, along the course the forecast plan sails: that course with perfect foresight, then the speed on the forecast with the kite launched and landed on the real weather, then its launches on the forecast, then the course re-planned on later forecasts instead of sailed as a fixed track. The last block is the kite's time near its limits and its launches, with perfect foresight and on the forecast plan. Method study, run 2026-11-13T1554 at draft fidelity, commit 3cd96bb \stale {forecast_causes}{}}}{31}{table.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:forecast_causes}{{S13}{31}{Where each rig's cost saving goes on the forecast result's plan, which weighs the wind error on a grid fine near the ship, re-planned after each edge of about a day on the archived forecasts and sailed at the plan's speed, for the \SI {140}{m} e-ammonia ship to Guadeloupe, and the kite's excess over the wing sails and over the rotors, paired per departure, each with its standard error over 24 departures. The rotors pair 22 of them, since two of their round trips found no route. Cost savings are against the ship without a rig on the same plan. The loss is split by changing one thing at a time: from perfect foresight on the corridor of the results to perfect foresight on the planning grid, then to the forecast; the forecast's loss by what it gets wrong, the wave or the wind forecast first; and by what the plan decides, along the course the forecast plan sails: that course with perfect foresight, then the speed on the forecast with the kite launched and landed on the real weather, then its launches on the forecast, then the course re-planned on later forecasts instead of sailed as a fixed track. The last block is the kite's time near its limits and its launches, with perfect foresight and on the forecast plan. Method study, \studyrun {forecast_causes} \stale {forecast_causes}{}}{table.13}{}}
\newlabel{tab:forecast_causes@cref}{{[table][13][]S13}{[1][29][]31}{}{}{}}
